<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix</h2>

</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Sorting: Experimental Details</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Bitonic Sorting Network</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Bitonic sorting networks (Batcher’s Algorithm) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Batcher, 1968</a>)</cite> allow us to optimize the sorting of a given list.
The comparisons in a bitonic sorting network are not data-dependent,
allowing for more feasible parallelization and speed proportional to depth <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given an input list, a bitonic sorting network originally sorts both halves of the list,
subsequently conducts an odd-even mergesort on both halves of the list and finally switches adjacent keys.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A sequence <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0},\ldots,a_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0},\ldots,a_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> elements is called <em id="A1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">bitonic</em> when, for some <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> (called an <em id="A1.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inflection point</em>),
<math id="A1.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0}\leq a_{1}...\leq a_{i}\geq a_{i+1}...\geq a_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0}\leq a_{1}...\leq a_{i}\geq a_{i+1}...\geq a_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
(i.e., the first part up to <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> is monotonically increasing and the second part is monotonically decreasing).
Assume <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is a power of 2
and a sequence <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{0},\ldots,a_{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{0},\ldots,a_{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is bitonic with inflection point <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=\frac{n}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=\frac{n}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, we construct two sequences <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with a following compare-and-exchange process:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\min(a_{0},a_{\frac{n}{2}}),\min(a_{1},a_{\frac{n}{2}+1}),...\min(a_{\frac{n}{2}-1},a_{n-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\min(a_{0},a_{\frac{n}{2}}),\min(a_{1},a_{\frac{n}{2}+1}),...\min(a_{\frac{n}{2}-1},a_{n-1})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\max(a_{0},a_{\frac{n}{2}}),\max(a_{1},a_{\frac{n}{2}+1}),...\max(a_{\frac{n}{2}-1},a_{n-1})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\max(a_{0},a_{\frac{n}{2}}),\max(a_{1},a_{\frac{n}{2}+1}),...\max(a_{\frac{n}{2}-1},a_{n-1}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are both bitonic sequences.
Moreover, any element in <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are larger than any element in <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,
<math id="A1.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall x\forall y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall x\forall y</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&lt;y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&lt;y</annotation></semantics></math>.
The recursive compare-and-exchange process above is applied on <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(n)</annotation></semantics></math> times to sort both halves of the list.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For some input sizes,
there are optimal sorting networks which require less comparisons or less parallel steps than Bitonic sorter does <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Knuth, 1997</a>)</cite>.
Bitonic sorting network can switch to such a network during the recursion,
further increasing the performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Kendall-Tau Score</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Kendall Tau correlation coefficient <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kendall (1945)</a></cite>
is a non-parameteric <em id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">statistic</em> that measures how much order is preserved between two sets of rankings.
Kendall-Tau score has several ways to compute (<math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{a},\tau_{b},\tau_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{a},\tau_{b},\tau_{c}</annotation></semantics></math>) depending on the existence of ties or the nature of the observations.
Here we describe <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> we used in our paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume each observation consists of an input variable <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and an output variable <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider two observations <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{i},Y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{i},Y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{j},Y_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{j},Y_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.
These observations are called <em id="A1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">concordant</em> if <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}&lt;X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}&lt;X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}&lt;Y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}&lt;Y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}&gt;X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}&gt;X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}&gt;Y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}&gt;Y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Conversely, they are called <em id="A1.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">discordant</em> if <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}&lt;X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}&lt;X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}&gt;Y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}&gt;Y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, or <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}&gt;X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}&gt;X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}&lt;Y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}&lt;Y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{i}=X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{i}=X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and/or <math id="A1.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{i}=Y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{i}=Y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, the observations are called <em id="A1.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tied</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> observations <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>O</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O</annotation></semantics></math>, enumerate all <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{n\choose 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{n\choose 2}</annotation></semantics></math> pairs, which we denote as <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="A1.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is then comprised of
concordant pairs <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>,
discordant pairs <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>,
pairs tied on the <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> variable denoted as <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>,
pairs tied on the <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> variable denoted as <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math>,
as well as pairs tied on both variables denoted as <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
Kendall <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is then defined as:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}=\frac{|P|-|Q|}{\sqrt{(|P|+|Q|+|T|)*(|P|+|Q|+|U|)}}\in[-1,1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>U</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mfrac><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}=\frac{|P|-|Q|}{\sqrt{(|P|+|Q|+|T|)*(|P|+|Q|+|U|)}}\in[-1,1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">A <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates a negative correlation between two rankings and
A <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> close to <math id="A1.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates aligned rankings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>LLM Query Prompts</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All methods uses a common shared criteria for comparison.
In the Amazon dataset used in the study, the criteria prompt is:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A1.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.SS3.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">criteria = ‘‘You will be given two product reviews, X and Y, written by various customers about the same product. Is X more favorable to the product than Y?’’</span></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We used the following query prompts:</p>
<dl id="A1.I1" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="A1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A1.I1.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Constrained-decoding baseline:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">f"Sort the following list in the increasing order of quality
that is compared in the following comparison criteria:
’{criteria}’.
That is, for a pair X and Y, if the answer to the criteria is ’yes’,
X must appear after Y in the output list. "</span></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="A1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A1.I1.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I.i.d Scoring:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">f"I want to sort several strings.
Given two strings X and Y, my comparison criteria is as follows:
’{criteria}’.
Assign a score to X below so that for any pair of strings X and Y,
if the answer to the comparison criteria above is yes,
the score of X is greater than the score of Y.
Each score must be between 1 and 5 with one decimal place,
where the score 5 means that X will most certainly satisfy the comparison against other strings,
and the score 1 means the opposite.
For example, if we compare the height of mountains, score 5 will be assigned to very tall mountains,
and if we compare the quality of restaurants, score 1 implies that the food or the service is very bad.
\nX: "</span>
followed by a string.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="A1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A1.I1.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Autoregressive Scoring:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">f"I want to sort several strings listed below.
Given two strings X and Y,
my comparison criteria is as follows: ’{criteria}’.
Assign a score to each string so that
for any pair of strings X and Y,
if the answer to the comparison criteria above is yes, the score of X is greater than the score of Y.
Return the scores as a space-separated list.
Each score must be between 1 and 5 with one decimal place,
where the score 5 means that X will most certainly satisfy the comparison against other strings,
and the score 1 means the opposite.
For example, if we compare the height of mountains, score 5 will be assigned to very tall mountains,
and if we compare the quality of restaurants, score 1 implies that the food or the service is very bad.
\n
strings:"
</span>
followed by a markdown-formatted list of strings.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="A1.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A1.I1.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verbalized Sorting:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A1.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I1.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">f"{criteria} Answer with a single word, yes or no. X: ’{self.string}’ Y: ’{other.string}’"
</span></p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Clustering: Experimental Details</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Jaccard distance</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This implements a baseline clustering method using lexical similarity.
Let <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the set of tokens from a string <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}\in S</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any two strings <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with corresponding token sets <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>,
the Jaccard distance is computed as:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J(s_{i},s_{j})=1-\frac{|T_{i}\cap T_{j}|}{|T_{i}\cup T_{j}|}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J(s_{i},s_{j})=1-\frac{|T_{i}\cap T_{j}|}{|T_{i}\cup T_{j}|}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|T_{i}\cap T_{j}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T_{i}\cap T_{j}|</annotation></semantics></math> represents the cardinality of the intersection
and <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|T_{i}\cup T_{j}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|T_{i}\cup T_{j}|</annotation></semantics></math> represents the cardinality of the union.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>t-STE</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Van Der Maaten and Weinberger (2012)</a></cite> proposed
a heavy-tailed kernel to measure local similarities between data points.
In particular, we opt to use a Student-<math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> kernel with <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> degrees of freedom by defining:</p>
<table id="A2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{ij\ell}=\frac{\left(1+\frac{\lVert x_{i}-x_{j}\rVert^{2}}{\alpha}\right)^{-\tfrac{\alpha+1}{2}}}{\left(1+\frac{\lVert x_{i}-x_{j}\rVert^{2}}{\alpha}\right)^{-\tfrac{\alpha+1}{2}}+\left(1+\frac{\lVert x_{i}-x_{\ell}\rVert^{2}}{\alpha}\right)^{-\tfrac{\alpha+1}{2}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></msup><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>α</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mfrac><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{ij\ell}=\frac{\left(1+\frac{\lVert x_{i}-x_{j}\rVert^{2}}{\alpha}\right)^{-\tfrac{\alpha+1}{2}}}{\left(1+\frac{\lVert x_{i}-x_{j}\rVert^{2}}{\alpha}\right)^{-\tfrac{\alpha+1}{2}}+\left(1+\frac{\lVert x_{i}-x_{\ell}\rVert^{2}}{\alpha}\right)^{-\tfrac{\alpha+1}{2}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We refer to the resulting technique as t-Distributed STE (t-STE).
Our implementation sets <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=D-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=D-1</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of dimensions in the resulting embedding.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The formulation of the triplet probabilities is motivated
by the success of unsupervised dimensionality reduction technique
that also employ heavy-tailed similarity kernels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maaten and Hinton, 2008</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>LLM Query Prompts</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Triplet comparison query uses the following shared prompt,
where <span id="A2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">criteria</span> is a dataset-specific prompt.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">f"{criteria} \nX: t.x\nY: t.y\nZ: t.z\n Answer either ’X’ or ’Y’." </span></p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The dataset-specific prompt is as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="A2.I1" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="A2.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A2.I1.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Medarxiv dataset:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A2.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">criteria = f"You will be given three paper titles X, Y, and Z.
Does the paper Z discuss a topic that is closer to the topic in X than to the topic in Y?"</span></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="A2.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A2.I1.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">IT Defect dataset:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A2.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">criteria = f"You will be given three defect reports X, Y, and Z,
sent by the customers of our GPFS filesystem storage system.
They may contain stack traces of various programs (python, kernel drivers, ...).
Similar repetitive lines are omitted as [...].
Considering the cause of the defects (i.e. ignoring timestamp/IP/MAC addresses),
are X and Z more similar than Y and Z are? "</span></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="A2.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A2.I1.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">xkcdcolors-h dataset:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A2.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">criteria = f"Which of the following two colors, X and Y, is more similar to the color Z?
Focus on the hue in the HSL model first, then other aspects such as lightness and saturation.
For example, Red and Pink are more similar than Red and Blue are.
If you are not familiar with the given color name, e.g., a made-up color name like ’puke brown’,
try to imagine what the color would look like, e.g., puke would generally have a lighter shade. "</span></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="A2.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="A2.I1.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">xkcdcolors-l dataset:</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="A2.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I1.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">criteria = f"Which of the following two colors, X and Y, is more similar to the color Z?
Focus on the lightness in the HSL model first, then other aspects such as hue and saturation.
For example, Red and Blue (both lightness = 50
If you are not familiar with the given color name, e.g., a made-up color name like ’puke brown’,
try to imagine what the color would look like, e.g., puke would generally have a lighter shade. "</span></p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>IT Defect Reports Dataset</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of all defects.
A subset <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}\subseteq D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>⊆</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}\subseteq D</annotation></semantics></math> was manually labeled as duplicates of several other defects in <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>.
We selected 78 data points so that the final dataset contains 25 such clusters, each of size at least 2.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Since these labels are directional and sparse,
we define the cluster by making the link undirected and
computing the connected components,
then treating a set of elements in each connected component as a cluster.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The following is a redacted/simplified defect records that appear in the dataset.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Defect 1</h4>

<div id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para"><pre id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
{
  "id": 347852,
  "description":
  "System login:
    EMS - X.XX.XXX.XXX root/cluster
    S6K w/ Falcon attached - lothal-qa6-[1 &amp;&amp; 2] Code levels:
    S6k - 6230_B19
    FCM4 - 4_3_3
    Falcon - 5342 --&gt; 5343

   Snap location:
    glogin:/u/DUMPS/R.347852/

   What I was doing:
    I was upgrading Falcon enclosure from 5342 to 5343.

   What I expected:
    ’XXXXfirmware’ will start and the Falcon will go through the upgrade process.

   What happened:
    ’mmchrimware’ completed but there are errors printed.
    The Falcon does appear to have the updated firmware.
    However, there are issues when trying to get a print
    from the Left module when running ’essenclosures’.

   Analysis:
    sg91:13:/dev/sg91::naa.500093d006585000/10910::[1.0.91.0]:/dev/sg91:XXX:5149-091:5342
    [10:48:31.258608008] XXXXXXFirmwareVer2 checking if enclosure needs to be updated
    [10:48:31.260567945] XXXXXXFirmwareVer2 firmware file found
    [10:48:31.262950549] XXXXXXFirmwareVer2 -&gt; ...
    [10:48:31.350737778] XXXXXXFirmwareVer2 ...
    [10:48:31.362309567] XXXXXXFirmwareVer2 lothal-qa6-1-hs: [E] Error 1 updating firmware ...
    sg_ses failed: Transport error
    ...",
  "duplicate_of": [],
  ...
}
</pre>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Defect 2</h4>

<div id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This is marked as a duplicate of the previous defect.</p><pre id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
{
  "id": 350018,
  "description": "Starting point:
    [root@s6krw3a ~]# XXXXfirmware --type storage-enclosure enclosure firmware ...
    enclosure 5149-091 78C100G 5342,5342 *5343 s6krw3a-hs enclosure 5149-F48 ...
    [root@s6krw3a ~]#

    Scenario:
    Storage enclosure fw update on 1 Falcon enclosure and 1 6k head unit. Build RC4
    [root@s6krw3a ~]# date; time XXXXfirmware --type storage-enclosure
    Thu May 29 05:26:19 PM EDT 2025
    Thu May 29 05:26:19 PM EDT 2025: XXXXfirmware: Processing node s6krw3a-hs....
    Thu May 29 05:26:23 PM EDT 2025: XXXXfirmware: Processing node s6krw3a-hs....
    s6krw3a-hs: update-directory .
    s6krw3a-hs: [I] Found storage-enclosure firmware update-id firmwareTable ...
    s6krw3a-hs: Updating enclosure firmware ESM_A.
    ...
    s6krw3a-hs: [E] Error 1 updating firmware for enclosure sg2.
    inquiry: transport: Host_status=0x0b [DID_SOFT_ERROR] /dev/sg2 doesn’t respond to a SCSI INQUIRY
    sg_ses failed: Transport error
    XXXXfirmware: Command failed. Examine previous error messages to determine cause.
    real 11m56.451s
    user 0m35.035s
    sys 2m7.818s
    [root@s6krw3a ~]#

    But apparently it still worked on canister A, but not on canister B…

    ...",
  "duplicate_of": [
    347852
  ],
  ...
}
</pre>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
</article>
</div>
